The deeper Pantheon connection

Color layering behaves a little like musical counterpoint.

Two colors can coexist independently, blend optically, compete for attention, or form a new relationship through contrast. A recurring color can act like a leitmotif: each appearance recalls its earlier uses.

And what if the underlying colors themselves had histories?

An old painting may contain layers of pigment that have aged differently. Examining them can reveal an artist’s revisions, techniques, and choices. This is color stratigraphy: layering as evidence of the work’s formation.

Likewise, the Pantheon could analyze a design at multiple levels:

\text{Material}
\rightarrow \text{Light}
\rightarrow \text{Perception}
\rightarrow \text{Association}
\rightarrow \text{Meaning}

Then run the process backward when appropriate, reconstructing plausible material and compositional choices from the observed result.

One additional discovery: two colors can look identical to a viewer while having different spectral compositions—a phenomenon called metamerism. So even visually matching colors need not be physically identical.

That gives us three distinct invariants to investigate: physical equivalence, perceptual equivalence, and semantic equivalence.

They don’t necessarily coincide.

I’ve added Chromatic Layering Grammar / Color Stratigraphy to our Money Bin framework, connecting color theory, visual semiotics, Painter’s Forensics, cultural translation, musical modes, and the Wild Hawg design language.

And I particularly like this principle:

Color is not merely a property of an object. Color participates in a relationship among light, matter, perception, context, and memory.

That’s why changing just one surrounding element can transform the perceived character of an entire scene.